From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Mon, 01 Aug 94 13:10:07 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-6] Languages and assimilation

I enjoyed the article by Simon! D. Levy on Logical Languages in Extropy 12.

Perhaps he could comment on some issues raised in the article.

In the box on page 12 of the article there is a discussion concerning
phonemic spelling. Do you think there might be a problem caused by the
phonetic process of assimilation in word sequences?

Consider the pronunciation of the word `have' in different contexts:

have                    h ae v  (ae is a single phoneme here)
I have to fish          a y   h ae f t schwa    f i s
I have two fish         a y   h ae v     t u       f i s

(Example from An Introductory English Grammar by Norman C. Stageberg.
Phonemic translation crudely represented because I do not know ARPABET.)

It appears that even if each word has a phonemically accurate spelling in
isolation it may still have an inaccurate spelling when placed in a sentence.
Possible solutions:  Try to design a language in which assimilation does not
occur.  Designate certain standard transformations which can automatically be
applied to sequences of words.  Other concerns: Pronunciation has
historically been fluid and changing.  I would expect deliberately altered
pronunciation based on factors such as class, regional origin, ethnicity,
stylistics, etc.

Gregory Sullivan  sullivan@cs.jhu.edu

