Subject: [#94-8-7] SELF: my heart (pumping)

Hi, all.

Anton asked about factors which might 
cause a reduction in his pulse.  Responses to 
this came from Eric Forste (reduction in 
caloric intake) and Mike Price (vitamin 
supplementation).  Each of these may result 
in a reduction in pulse, and there are many 
other lifestyle modifications which could also 
contribute.

Your health may be affected by both physical 
and psychological changes.  From the 
physical angle, caloric-intake reduction, and 
vitamins (as mentioned), exercise, stopping 
smoking, and moving from a higher altitude 
to a lower are just a few examples of things which 
may reduce your pulse rate over time.  
Anything which improves your heart's ability 
to circulate oxygen and nutrients could be a 
factor (i.e. heart muscle strength, higher 
percentage of oxygen/nutrients per unit 
blood, greater diameter available in the 
vasculature for blood transportation (as with reduced 
arterioschlerosis), more efficient 
oxygen/nutrient uptake, etc.).  Given the 
complexity of the human body, it is difficult 
to determine conclusively what multitude of 
factors might improve the pumping action of 
the heart, but examining potential factors 
with respect to the function/purpose of the 
heart will help.

Psychological factors also affect heart action, 
in that stress affects the body's demand for 
sustinance.  Improving your self-
understanding or self-esteem, falling in love, 
implementing effective relaxation techniques 
(i.e. meditation, breathing exercises), 
optimizing your home and work 
environments for happiness and productivity, 
or having a pet are some things which might 
also reduce the stress on your heart.  
(Personally, I recommend cats.  They've 
worked well for me. :-)

It is important to remember that it is the 
long-term trend which must be analysed 
when considering physiologic changes 
(annual evaluation may be insufficient, under 
cicumstances where you've experienced 
severe trauma with the year).  The body has 
a tremendous resiliency, but hard work and 
dedication are essential if you wish to 
optimize your healthy, long-term survival.  
Of course, there are genetically-advantaged 
individuals, but that's beyond my control to 
change.  (I'd like to hear from anyone who's 
capable of these sorts of changes!)  It's my 
opinion that the most effective strategy for 
optimizing health includes evaluation and 
modification, if necessary, of both the 
physical and psychological components of 
life.

Hope this helps.

--Tanya

