From: dasher@netcom.com (Anton Sherwood)
Date: Mon, 1 Aug 1994 23:22:36 -0700
Subject: [#94-8-8] iterated fuzzy prisoner's dilemma

Looking for gray IPD strategies that tend to move toward cooperation
even if the history is random.  This to better model the real world,
with noise and prejudice.

(Again: 0=defect, 1=cooperate.)

The "stickleback" strategy for classic IPD is: x = x1 xor ~y1.
(x1 = the previous cycle's x, etc.)  This recovers from defection
in 2 cycles.  A gray or fuzzy version using the bit-string
representation (.0000 <= x,y <= .1111) is effectively a series
of parallel games.

An analog version strikes me as more interesting.  The theory of
"stickleback" is: If what I did before isn't working, change it.
But if x1=1/2, what to change _to_?  And when to change, how
much?  Here's an idea:  x = (y1 * x1) + (~y1 * rand(0,1)).
This should work well with Tit for Tat, unlike the binary form.

