(MOURNING) RITUALS

Harry Hawk urges us to find a "rational mourning ritual" for dealing with a
loved one's deanimation.  We as human beings have all experienced some form of
loss or emotional suffering.  How is the extropian to deal with such situations?

Traditional bio-memetic co-evolution has yeilded a wide range of rituals for
returning a human to a functional state (out of depression or mourning).
Unfortunately, many of these are associated with religious connotations that
many of us find less than satisfactory.  These techniques have been tested over
time and Harry has pointed out that the Jewish tradition of tearing of clothes
and constant attention on the mourner is an ESS as I'm sure many other
traditions are.

There was the advent to pschoanalysis to replace religious techniques.  This is
a form of MENTAL DEBUGGING.

So there are perhaps two routes for the futurist: Change the brain or Placate
the brain.

1)  Changing the brain would mean just turning off the "mental pain" 
	(Prozac anyone?) 
2)  Placating the brain would mean creating modern rituals or mental debugging 
techniques.
	(Freudian TechnoRave anyone?)

I'm curious about extropians' attitudes.

Going to Museums or taking a bicycle ride is a waste of time (when we could
be working instead of playing).  Should we shuffle off our silly pleasures in
our transhumanist ascent, or do we carry with us nostalgic biological
memorabelia? 

